  <front>
    <title abbrev="vector/core/hex">vector/core/hex — hex and SHA-256 helpers</title>
    <seriesInfo name="Internet-Draft" value="draft-vector-core-hex-00"/>
    <author initials="V." surname="Authors" fullname="Vector Authors">
      <organization>vector</organization>
      <address>
      </address>
    </author>
    <date year="2026" month="November" day="13"/>
    <abstract>
      <?line 13?>

<t>Module: src/core/hex.ts (vector/core/hex), vector's own. The helpers every
command shares to name bytes: toHex writes bytes as lowercase hex, and
sha256Hex names a text by its SHA-256 digest, the hash a contract records for
each source it quotes. vector checks itself with this spec: its test is
emitted into tests/dogfood/ and runs with npm test.</t>
      <t>This spec leaves two things out. It does not cover utf8Bytes, because the
emitted test defines a helper with that name. It does not cover text outside
ASCII, because a string domain or quoted string in a spec can hold only
printable ASCII.</t>
    </abstract>
  </front>
  <middle>
    <?line 26?>

<section anchor="introduction">
      <name>Introduction</name>
      <t>The key words "<bcp14>MUST</bcp14>", "<bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>REQUIRED</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHALL</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHALL
NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHOULD</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHOULD NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>RECOMMENDED</bcp14>", "<bcp14>NOT RECOMMENDED</bcp14>",
"<bcp14>MAY</bcp14>", and "<bcp14>OPTIONAL</bcp14>" in this document are to be interpreted as
described in BCP 14 <xref target="RFC2119"/> <xref target="RFC8174"/> when, and only when, they
appear in all capitals, as shown here.</t>
      <?line -18?>

<t>A contract pins every source it quotes by sha256Hex of the source's text, and
an emitted test names its contract the same way, so a wrong digest or a
digest spelled in another case would make every frozen check fail or, worse,
pass on the wrong bytes. This spec pins the three helpers down.</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="interface">
      <name>Interface</name>
      <sourcecode type="ts-interface"><![CDATA[
// module: vector/core/hex
export const toHex = (bytes: u8[]): string
export const sha256Hex = (text: string): string
]]></sourcecode>
    </section>
    <section anchor="hex">
      <name>Hex</name>
      <section anchor="hex-length">
        <name>Length</name>
        <t anchor="R-HEX-LEN">toHex <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> return exactly two characters per input byte.</t>
        <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-HEX-LEN covers R-HEX-LEN — two characters per byte
forall (b: u8[] /* 0..64 */) => toHex(b).length === 2 * b.length
]]></sourcecode>
      </section>
      <section anchor="hex-digits">
        <name>Digits</name>
        <t anchor="R-HEX-DIGITS">toHex <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> write each byte high nibble first, using only the digits 0-9 and
a-f.</t>
        <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-HEX-LOWER covers R-HEX-DIGITS — the encoding has no uppercase digit
forall (b: u8[] /* 0..64 */) => {
  const e = toHex(b);
  return e === e.toLowerCase();
}
]]></sourcecode>
        <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-HEX-CONCAT covers R-HEX-LEN, R-HEX-DIGITS — each byte is written on its own, in order
forall (a: u8[] /* 0..32 */, b: u8[] /* 0..32 */) => toHex(a.concat(b)) === toHex(a) + toHex(b)
]]></sourcecode>
      </section>
    </section>
    <section anchor="sha-256">
      <name>SHA-256</name>
      <t anchor="R-SHA256">sha256Hex <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> return the SHA-256 digest of the UTF-8 encoding of its text,
as FIPS 180-4 defines SHA-256, written as toHex writes bytes.</t>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-SHA256-LEN covers R-SHA256 — a digest is 64 lowercase hex digits
forall (t: string /* 0..80 ascii-printable */) => {
  const d = sha256Hex(t);
  return d.length === 64 && d === d.toLowerCase();
}
]]></sourcecode>
    </section>
    <section anchor="security-considerations">
      <name>Security Considerations</name>
      <t>sha256Hex names the bytes a contract was frozen against; a collision in it
would let a changed source pass the frozen check. SHA-256 is chosen for that.</t>
    </section>
